// wholesale pack -- the writes (epic memql#5533, issues memql#5555,
// memql#5556 and memql#5557).
//
// # Why these exist when every ENTRY POINT is a builtin
//
// A BUILTIN COMPUTES; IT DOES NOT WRITE. component/memql's
// evaluateBuiltinFunctionExpression returns its handler's nodes as the
// expression's VALUE and persists nothing -- so a pack whose writes were
// "the node a builtin returned" would report success, write nothing, and
// answer zero on the very next read. Nothing errors anywhere. The live
// e2e suite is what found that, and no fixture-backed test could have.
//
// So the shape is: THE BUILTIN DECIDES, THE MUTATION WRITES. Each
// capability makes the cross-row reads a mutation body cannot -- is this
// store accepting applications, what store is this application on, what
// does the decision log fold to, is this transition legal -- and then
// calls the matching mutation here through the engine, under the caller's
// own actor (or, from the store's automation, the application's merchant).
//
// # Every one of these is @serverOnly
//
// Each is reached ONLY from the builtin that judges it, through reader.go's
// engineWriter, which stamps internal origin for that one call. A client
// calling one directly would write around the check that makes the write
// legal -- a decision appended past the legality fold, an entitlement
// nobody approved -- so the engine refuses it. The caller's actor still
// runs the mutation, so the row gate still decides whether it may write.
//
// # ownerUserId is an ARGUMENT, accepted
//
// Every concept in this pack is @rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId",
// clusterOwner), so a row's owner is the merchant it belongs to. The Go
// names that merchant -- the borrowed site owner on submission, the
// application's ownerUserId on a decision and an entitlement -- because
// the actor running a write is not always that merchant: provisioning from
// the store's automation reads as the deployment, and a row stamped from a
// system actor is owned by nobody.

use wholesale.concepts.{ application, applicationDecision, entitlement, wholesaleSettings }

/// Write one application. IT NAMES NO ROW ID: on the shopper path the
/// caller is a member of the public, `id` is a name the shopper surface
/// refuses outright, and a shopper who could choose a row id could
/// overwrite another applicant's. The engine derives one.
/// ownerUserId is the site owner whose authority the shopper handler
/// borrowed, which is the calling actor.
@serverOnly
@actor
mutation application createApplicationRow {
  args {
    applicationId   string!
    ownerUserId     string!
    storeId         string!
    siteId          string
    companyName     string!  @maxLength(200)
    applicantName   string!  @maxLength(120)
    applicantEmail  string!  @maxLength(320)
  }
  insert {
    accept { ownerUserId, storeId, siteId, companyName, applicantName, applicantEmail }
    stamp {
      id: args.applicationId
    }
  }
}

/// Append one decision. ALSO NAMES NO ID, because a decision is a new row
/// every time: append-only is what keeps why an application was refused
/// readable after the next decision, and an id the caller chose would let
/// a second decision overwrite the first.
///
/// decidedBy IS THE ACTOR AND IS NOT AN ARGUMENT. The builtin in front of
/// this takes one -- the issue's own wording, and reviewspack's moderation
/// capability does the same -- but the ROW records who actually called,
/// because a caller-supplied decider on an APPEND-ONLY log is a way to
/// attribute somebody else's approval to them permanently, with no later
/// write able to correct it. When the two disagree the actor wins, which
/// is the only one of them that cannot be forged. ownerUserId is the
/// application's merchant, copied off it by the builtin.
@serverOnly
@actor
mutation applicationDecision appendApplicationDecision {
  args {
    applicationId  string!
    ownerUserId    string
    storeId        string!
    transition     string!  @enum("approve", "reject", "revoke")
    note           string   @maxLength(2000)
  }
  insert {
    accept { applicationId, ownerUserId, storeId, transition, note }
    stamp {
      decidedBy: actor.userId
      principalKind: "client"
    }
  }
}

/// Write one store's settings. NAMES ITS ID, derived from the store, so
/// there is exactly one row per store and a second write is a new VERSION
/// of it rather than a second row a read would have to choose between.
@serverOnly
@actor
mutation wholesaleSettings setWholesaleSettingsRow {
  args {
    settingsId          string!
    storeId             string!
    applicationsOpen    bool!
    entitlementAdapter  string
    introText           string   @maxLength(2000)
  }
  insert {
    accept { storeId, applicationsOpen, entitlementAdapter, introText }
    stamp {
      id: args.settingsId
      ownerUserId: actor.userId
    }
  }
}

/// Write one application's entitlement. NAMES ITS ID, derived from the
/// application, so a re-provision after a failure is a new version of one
/// logical row -- and "revoked when" is that version's own createdAt,
/// which is why the concept carries no revokedAt. ownerUserId is the
/// application's merchant: the write runs as that merchant, and a row owned
/// by anybody else is invisible to the merchant who granted it.
@serverOnly
@actor
mutation entitlement writeEntitlementRow {
  args {
    entitlementId  string!
    ownerUserId    string!
    applicationId  string!
    storeId        string!
    buyerEmail     string!
    adapter        string!
    state          string!  @enum("pending", "granted", "failed", "revoked")
    reference      string
    error          string   @maxLength(4000)
  }
  insert {
    accept { ownerUserId, applicationId, storeId, buyerEmail, adapter, state, reference, error }
    stamp {
      id: args.entitlementId
    }
  }
}
